BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//Monash Events//EN
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:CHE Seminar Series: Mother nature\, mother nurture - Intergenerati
 onal persistence in two-mother families
DTSTART:20260826T120000
DTEND:20260826T130000
DESCRIPTION:Why do children of more educated mothers tend to do better in 
 education themselves? We propose a novel strategy to answer this question 
 using unique data from the Netherlands. By comparing donor-conceived child
 ren raised in two-mother families\, we disentangle the influences of biolo
 gical and social motherhood. We find that children's test scores are more 
 strongly associated with the education of their biological mother than tha
 t of their co-mother. Strikingly\, even within the same family (where chil
 dren are raised by the same two mothers)\, those with the more educated bi
 ological mother perform substantially better. These estimates capture the 
 combined effects of genetic endowments\, in-utero conditions\, and early m
 other–child interactions. We find no evidence that children of more educ
 ated mothers are healthier at birth\, and only limited evidence that they 
 receive greater maternal investments in early childhood. Taken together\, 
 our results point to a prominent role for education-related heritable fact
 ors in explaining intergenerational persistence.\n\nSpeaker profile\n\n<a 
 href="https://sanderdevries.github.io/" target="_blank">Sander de Vries</a
 > is an economist whose research focuses on economic inequality\, intergen
 erational mobility\, and the economics of education. He uses large adminis
 trative datasets together with causal inference and machine learning metho
 ds to study these topics. He is currently a postdoctoral researcher at the
  University of Technology Sydney\, where he contributes to the Australian 
 Research Council Discovery Project Inequality and Intergenerational Mobili
 ty: Measuring What Matters and Why\, alongside Peter Siminski\, Nathan Deu
 tscher\, Roger Wilkins\, and Bhaskar Mazumder.\n\nWeekly seminar series\n
 \nAs part of our Centre's vibrant research culture\, we host a weekly semi
 nar series. Visiting and invited researchers present current research rela
 ting to the economics of health and wellbeing\, and the healthcare sector.
  Visitors are welcome to join these sessions where discussion and debate i
 s encouraged.\n\nFor further information on our seminar series\, please co
 ntact <a href="mailto:Trong-Anh.Trinh@monash.edu">Trong-Anh.Trinh@monash.
 edu</a>\n\n<a class="btn" href="https://monash.zoom.us/j/83600802355?pwd=1
 F4DXlfQDO7MHpjzEWsMC6dZbAX5do.1%20">Join Zoom</a>
LOCATION:Caulfield campus\, Building H\, level 9\, room H9.21
URL:https://www.monash.edu/business/events/che-seminar-series-mother-natur
 e,-mother-nurture-intergenerational-persistence-in-two-mother-families
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Centre for Health Economics Policy Symposium 2026
DTSTART:20260901T093000
DTEND:20260901T170000
DESCRIPTION:<em>Sustainable Care: The next decade of policy reform</em>\n
 \nThis symposium brings together researchers\, policymakers\, practitioner
 s and consumers to examine how Australia’s health\, aged care and disabi
 lity systems need to evolve over the coming decade. The full-day conferenc
 e will focus on the future of the care economy\; exploring:\n\nCombined\, 
 the sessions aim to identify practical\, evidence‑based reforms to stren
 gthen the long‑term sustainability and effectiveness of care systems\n\n
 Registration\n\nRegistration is open. Early bird tickets are now sold out.
 \n\n<a class="btn" href="https://events.humanitix.com/centre-for-health-ec
 onomics-policy-symposium-2026">Register</a>\n\nWhat does the future of the
  care economy hold\, and how are health\, aged care\, and disability polic
 ies evolving to meet the challenges of tomorrow?\n\nHow can we optimise th
 e architecture of our care system? How can we balance universality\, means
 - testing\, and out-of-pocket costs to improve overall wellbeing?\n\nHow d
 o we define and measure productivity in a sector where the primary output 
 is wellbeing rather than just volume and profit?  How can better measure
 ment support accountability\, smarter decisions and more person-centred ca
 re?\n\nPrices can provide strong incentives for both patients and provider
 s. How should we set prices across the care economy? Can better price sign
 als lift quality\, reduce unnecessary care and improve the sustainability 
 of the system?\n\nReflection on the day and priorities for future research
 .\n\nHost\n\n<img alt="" height="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/a
 ssets/image/0004/4373482/Tony-Scott.png" style="float: right\; margin: 1em
  0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nPProf Scott’s goal is to influence heal
 th policy using high quality research.  He is a health economist and an
  elected Fellow of the Academy of the Social Sciences in Australia\, Past 
 President of the Australian Health Economics Society\, and a former Board 
 Director of the International Health Economics Association\, ARC Future Fe
 llow and NHMRC Principal Research Fellow.\n\nHis research expertise includ
 e the evaluation of national health policy\, the behaviour of healthcare p
 roviders\, health and care labour markets\, funding and incentives\, with 
 a focus on primary care. He has published in leading international academi
 c journals and impacted on policy through his academic and policy research
  and work on government committees\n\n<br/>Keynote Speaker\n\n<img alt="" 
 height="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/image/0005/4373483/
 250130_BillShortenPortraits_HiRes-1-1.jpg" style="float: right\; margin: 1
 em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nProf Shorten became Vice-Chancellor and
  President of the University of Canberra in January 2025\, after a career 
 that included leadership roles with the Australian Workers’ Union and 18
  years serving federal politics\, including six as Leader of the Oppositio
 n. Prof Shorten studied law at Monash University.<br/> <br/>Prof Shorten
 ’s belief in the transformative power of education drew him to UC and th
 e role. He arrived at a time of significant change for the university and 
 the broader higher education sector. It is a circumstance he views as an o
 pportunity to drive UC’s rejuvenation.\n\nSpeakers\n\n<img alt="" height
 ="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/image/0009/4373496/Blair.
 png" style="float: right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nMr 
 Comley commenced as the Secretary of the Department of Health\, Disability
  and Ageing on 17 July 2023. He has served as the Secretary of the Commonw
 ealth Department of Climate Change and Energy Efficiency and the Secretary
  of the Commonwealth Department of Resources\, Energy and Tourism. Mr Coml
 ey has also served as Secretary of the NSW Department of Premier and Cabin
 et\, NSW’s most senior public servant.\n\nPrior to these positions\, he 
 held senior leadership positions in the Department of Treasury and as the 
 Acting Chief Executive of the Australian Office of Financial Management\, 
 with responsibility for managing the Commonwealth’s debt and derivative 
 portfolio. Mr Comley has had extensive engagement with Commonwealth State 
 relations\, including reform of the Federation and Health funding in his r
 ole as Secretary of the Department of Premier and Cabinet in the NSW Gover
 nment.\n\n<img alt="" height="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/asse
 ts/image/0007/4373494/Stephen-Duckett.jpg" style="float: right\; margin: 1
 em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nProf Duckett is a health economist with
  expertise in costing and pricing hospital services and the design of hosp
 ital payment systems. He is an Honorary Enterprise Professor in the School
  of Population and Global Health and the Department of General Practice an
 d Primary Care at the University of Melbourne. He is also a Fellow of the 
 Academy of the Social Sciences in Australia\, the Australian Academy of He
 alth and Medical Sciences and the Australian Institute of Company Director
 s.<br/> <br/>Professor Duckett is also currently Deputy Chancellor of RMIT
  University. He was made a Member of the Order of Australia in January 202
 3.<br/> <br/>He has held top health sector leadership positions in Austral
 ia and Canada\, and literally wrote the book on the Australian health care
  system (<em>The Australian Health Care System</em>\, Oxford University Pr
 ess\, 6e\, 2022).\n\n<img alt="" height="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/
 __data/assets/image/0007/4373485/Elizabeth-dreaming-2.jpg" style="float: r
 ight\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nDr Deveny leads the CHF
 A\, which is the national voice for health consumers.\n\nShe has worked ac
 ross digital health\, primary care\, and health funding\, always with a fo
 cus on equity\, access\, and making sure policy reflects the voices of the
  people it affects — and while she’s not a health economist\, she know
 s value for money when she sees it.\n\n<img alt="" height="175" src="https
 ://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/image/0006/4373493/Emily-Lanscar.png" styl
 e="float: right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nProf Lancsar
  commenced as the Chief Health Economist in the department in April 2024. 
 Prior to this\, Prof Lancsar spent 25 years in academia\, most recently as
  Head of the Department of Health Economics Wellbeing and Society at the A
 ustralian National University (ANU). She also served as Associate Dean (Po
 licy and Practice) in the College of Health and Medicine at the ANU from 2
 020–2022\, where she remains a Professor.<br/> <br/>Prof Lancsar has als
 o held academic appointments at Monash University (where she remains an Ad
 junct Professor)\, the University of Newcastle Upon Tyne in the United Kin
 gdom\, the University of Sydney and University of Technology\, Sydney. Pro
 f Lancsar holds PhD\, Masters\, and Bachelors degrees in Economics\, a Pos
 tgrad Diploma in Health Economics and Evaluation\, and a Bachelor of Arts 
 (Asian Studies).\n\n<!-- false -->\n<div class="accordion nested-accordion
  js-frontend-complex-accordion" id="Component-4373352" role="tablist">\n<d
